    <section>
        <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
        <p>Current efforts to improve the quality of life for individuals with physical and sensory disabilities often focus on mobility aids. However, there is a critical gap in <strong>environmental spatial awareness</strong>. This research proposes the development of an Integrated Sensory Feedback System (ISFS) that uses augmented reality (AR) and haptic feedback to provide real-time navigation and environmental data to users, effectively creating a "digital layer" of accessibility in public spaces.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>2. Problem Statement</h2>
        <p>People with visual impairments or neurodivergent individuals often struggle with sudden environmental changes—such as construction, crowd surges, or unexpected architectural obstacles. The inability to predict these changes leads to increased anxiety, physical danger, and social isolation, lowering overall quality of life.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>3. Research Methodology</h2>
        <p>The project will be divided into three distinct phases:</p>
        <ul>
